# Monster level: no skull, no spread (server/README.md "Monster level: no skull, no spread (O326)"). Owner 2026-10-29
# (pt-BR): "monstros com caveira ainda estao muito fortes, tire a mecanica da caveira". Monster::Monster rolled a level
# uniformly in the <level min max> of a level-scaled type and then (a) set a skull by it (white 50-100, green 101-200,
# yellow 201-300, orange 301-350, red 351-400, black 401+) and (b) the level added health, speed and damage
# (config.lua monsterBonusHealth / Speed / Damage x level), so two monsters of one type differed by up to the whole range
# (a level 400 type: +0% to +296% health); the skull marked the high rolls. Now:
#   1. the skull block is gone: the skull is the one the XML <skull> gives (mType->info.skull) or a script sets later
#      (creature:setSkull, e.g. the nemesis look);
#   2. the level is the MIDDLE of the range, not a roll: every monster of a type has the same level, so the same health,
#      speed and damage bonus, equal to the mean the roll gave. Nothing that scales by the level changes on average
#      (pace curves, areagen / waystone / cursed chest scaling, the models); the spread is gone. The level is also what
#      is shown after the name (patches 0040 / 0044) and what getMonsterLevel() returns, now the same for the whole type.
# The config keys stay as they are: no config change, no data change. Applies to XML and Lua monster types alike.
# Guard: tools/monster_level_patch_test.py (CI). Applies after 0001-0081 (and after 0044 in particular).
